    "lawCode": "Rpld & add §35.03 sub 1 ¶(r), Pks & Rec L",
    "lawSection": "Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6678\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the parks, recreation and historic\npreservation law, in relation to establishing the Heights Heritage\nArea; and to repeal certain provisions of such law relating thereto\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nEstablishes the Heights Heritage Area, sets its boundaries and\nprovides for its historic development features.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Amends the Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation La\nby adding the heights Heritage area to those historic settings of the\nstate that have been identified for their statewide significance in\nthe plan for a statewide system of urban cultural parks; and sets the\nboundaries for the Heights Heritage area.\n\nSection 2. Provides that this act shall take effect on the one hundred\nand twentieth day.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nFor Northern Manhattan, the most important benefit of a state\ndesignated heritage area is the ability to safeguard a way of life and\npreserve history and neighborhood character.\n\nHistoric Harlem Heights was once a lush, natural region with flying\nhawks and wild turkeys. The Hudson River, with its picturesque banks\nforming gentle grassy slopes, was full of young salmon, large oysters\nand sturgeon. Deer, wolves and bears roamed the land freely. Native\nAmerican groups like the Reckgawawanc and Weckquaesgeeks lived in\nnumerous settlements throughout Northern Manhattan. Over the last 340\nyears, this area of Manhattan has gone through numerous\ntransformations, from sprawling farms, to villages with ferries\ndotting the Hudson, to clusters of urban neighborhoods with subways,\nwide boulevards and bridges.\n\nFor too long the contributions of Northern Manhattan to the history of\nNew York City and State have been neglected. The community is\nendeavoring to keep this history alive through State parks\ndesignation, which will allow the region to attract needed funds to\nimprove the public landscape, market and promote local businesses and\ninstitutions, and spur economic growth to attract new businesses and\nincrease tourism.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-2016: A.3486A\n2013-2014: A.5856 Passed Assembly\n2011-2012: A.2249 Passed Assembly\n2004: A.8724: Signed Chapter 688 of the Laws of 2004\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred and twentieth day after\nit shall become law.\n",
